  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 209 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nThe upper level pattern gets a bit complicated as we head into the \nweekend. An upper level low slowly tracks across the Great Lakes \nwith another trough tracking across the West Coast. This trough \nbegins to undergo a split flow regime, with a shortwave tracking \nacross the southern Plains while another shortwave clips north into \nthe Intermountain West. Latest guidance tracks the southern \nshortwave even further to the south, now expected to track across \nwestern Texas. The surface low associated with this system is \nexpected to remain well south, with the bulk of precipitation \nfalling across Kansas and Oklahoma. However, the very northern edge \nof this system may clip the very southwestern portions of Nebraska, \nbringing very light precipitation, if any at all. Ensembles continue \nto suggest that if precipitation were to fall, it would be a few \nhundredths at most. However, with light precipitation possible, may \nsee a few flurries mixing in, especially Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning. Again, not expecting anything impactful with this, but \nsomething to keep an eye on. This complex pattern is also expected \nto favor cooler temperatures across the region through the weekend, \nwith highs mostly remaining in the 40s. \n\nLooking ahead to early next week, upper level ridging builds in \nquickly for at least Monday, possibly into Tuesday. This will help \nusher in warming temperatures, with highs climbing back into the \n50s. This steady warming should start with near seasonal highs early \nweek, in the lower 50s, with temperatures rising above normal (highs \nin the mid to upper 50s) by mid week. Throughout the day Tuesday, a \nsecond upper level trough is expected to track across the Desert \nSouthwest, which brings potential for a surface low to develop by \nmid week. This system is currently expected to track across the \nregion, bringing potential for additional rain showers. Currently, \nensemble guidance brings a 30 to 40 percent chance for one tenth of \nan inch across portions of the region, with the European ensembles \nshowing the higher percentages for this occurring. Will continue to \nmonitor the forecast in the extended period to see if guidance comes \ninto better consensus.",
